---

00 — THESIS

Participation must feel simple.
Authority must remain constitutional.

De gebruiker ziet:
KIEZEN → BEVESTIGEN → HANDELEN

Het systeem bewaakt:
SCOPE → BOUNDARY → PROOF → REVIEW

---

01 — MASTER LOOP

ARRIVE
 ↓
UNDERSTAND
 ↓
SELECT ACTION
 ↓
PREVIEW CONSEQUENCE
 ↓
VERIFY IDENTITY CONTEXT
 ↓
REQUEST AUTHORIZATION
 ↓
EXECUTE OR DENY
 ↓
PROOF
 ↓
REVIEW
 ↓
RETURN

---

02 — CORE LAW

PREVIEW SHALL SHOW CONSEQUENCE BEFORE COMMITMENT.

No blind confirmation.
No hidden side effects.

---

03 — HUMAN ACTION SURFACES

SURFACE A — INTENT

"Wat wil je doen?"

SURFACE B — CONSEQUENCE

"Wat gebeurt er precies als je doorgaat?"

SURFACE C — DECISION

"Doorgaan / Annuleren / Bekijk bewijs"

SURFACE D — RESULT

"Uitgevoerd / Geweigerd / Onzeker"

---

04 — AUTHORITY BOUNDARY

The interface may:
- explain
- translate
- preview
- verify context
- submit request

The interface may not:
- self-authorize
- bypass gate
- fabricate approval
- mask denial reason

Canonical rule:

INTERACTION DOES NOT CONSTITUTE AUTHORITY.

---

05 — ACTION OBJECT

Every material action should expose:

WHAT
WHO
WHERE
WHY
SCOPE
EXPECTED IMPACT
NON-REVERSIBLE EFFECTS
DEPENDENCIES
REQUIRED AUTHORITY

---

06 — PREVIEW CONTRACT

Before confirmation, preview should show:

- what will change
- what will not change
- who can observe the change
- whether action is reversible
- which policy/gate applies
- what proof will be produced

---

07 — DECISION STATES

READY
BLOCKED
REQUIRES_REVIEW
PENDING
APPROVED
DENIED
EXECUTED
ROLLED_BACK

State labels must be explicit.

---

08 — DENIAL UX

When denied, show:

ACTION DENIED.

Reason:
- scope mismatch
- policy boundary
- missing prerequisite
- revoked authority

Next actions:
[ VIEW DETAILS ]
[ REQUEST REVIEW ]
[ RETURN ]

No vague denial.

---

09 — EXECUTION PROOF

After execution, produce:

ACTION ID
TIMESTAMP
ACTOR REF
CITADEL
STATUS
VERSION
PROVENANCE POINTER

Proof availability is part of completion.

---

10 — REVIEW FLOW

Every material action supports review path:

EXECUTION
 ↓
RECORD
 ↓
REVIEW REQUEST
 ↓
DECISION
 ↓
OUTCOME LOG

Review must be attributable and visible.

---

11 — REVOCATION FLOW

If authority is revoked:

- future actions are blocked
- in-flight actions get explicit status
- historical records remain intact

No historical erasure.

---

12 — IDENTITY LINKAGE

Action requests must bind to:

IDENTITY-ID
ROLE
CITADEL CONTEXT
VISIBILITY LEVEL
CURRENT STATUS

Identity presentation alone is insufficient;
verification context is required.

---

13 — STEWARDSHIP LINKAGE

For governed actions, expose:

STEWARD
AUTHORITY SCOPE
REVIEW CHANNEL
REVOCATION CHANNEL

Responsibility stays bounded.

---

14 — SAFETY LAYERS

LAYER 1: Intent clarity
LAYER 2: Consequence preview
LAYER 3: Identity context verification
LAYER 4: Authorization gate
LAYER 5: Proof emission
LAYER 6: Reviewability

Skipping layers is not allowed.

---

15 — ERROR ARCHITECTURE

UNKNOWN ACTION
Action could not be resolved.

UNVERIFIED CONTEXT
Identity or provenance context is incomplete.

GATE UNAVAILABLE
Authorization service temporarily unavailable.

All error states remain calm, factual, and actionable.

---

16 — ACCESSIBILITY

Participation flow must support:
- keyboard-only progression
- semantic status announcements
- focus-safe confirmation dialogs
- reduced motion for transitions
- non-color status distinctions

---

17 — SECURITY / TRUST UX

The UI must distinguish:

REQUESTED
vs
AUTHORIZED

AUTHORIZED
vs
EXECUTED

EXECUTED
vs
VERIFIED IN PROOF

No implied success chain.

